1. The original solution has a time complexity of O(n^2), which is inefficient for large values of n.
2. To optimize, we will use the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm, which has a time complexity of O(n log log n).
3. This algorithm efficiently finds all prime numbers up to a given limit by iteratively marking the multiples of each prime number starting from 2.

Pseudo-code:
```
function sieveOfEratosthenes(n):
    create a boolean array "isPrime" of size n+1 and initialize all entries as true
    set isPrime[0] and isPrime[1] to false
    for p from 2 to sqrt(n):
        if isPrime[p] is true:
            for multiple from p*p to n with step p:
                set isPrime[multiple] to false
    for i from 2 to n:
        if isPrime[i] is true:
            print i
```

Final Code:
import java.util.Arrays;

public class AllPrimeNumbers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 1000000;

        System.out.println("All Prime Numbers between 1 and " + n + " are:");
        sieveOfEratosthenes(n);
    }

    public static void sieveOfEratosthenes(int n) {
        boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[n + 1];
        Arrays.fill(isPrime, true);
        isPrime[0] = false;
        isPrime[1] = false;

        for (int p = 2; p * p <= n; p++) {
            if (isPrime[p]) {
                for (int multiple = p * p; multiple <= n; multiple += p) {
                    isPrime[multiple] = false;
                }
            }
        }

        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            if (isPrime[i]) {
                System.out.print(i + " ");
            }
        }
    }
}

